    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d173-a1">

                <pb facs="0214" n="164" xml:id="pg164"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.—Telegram.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chinese Consul-General at San
                            Francisco to Mr. Yang
                    Yü.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >San Francisco</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1894-04-10"><hi rendition="#italic">April 10,
                        1894</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p>The names mentioned in petition are Portland, Oreg.; El Paso and San Antonio, of
                    Texas; Los Angeles, Stanford, Marysville, and Santa Rosa, of California; Denver,
                    Colo., and Boston, Mass. Letters of complaint are still coming from El Paso and
                    San Antonio, of Texas, saying that the collectors there still refuse to register
                    Chinese unless they furnish at least one witness other than Chinese who has
                    known applicant for at least twelve years. Besides the above, letters have been
                    received from Marshfield, Oreg.; Rocky Bar, Elmore County, Idaho; Palmetto,
                    Esmeralda County, Nev.; Hot Springs, Ark.; Mountain City, Nev.; Union City,
                    Tenn.; Willcox, Ariz.; Burns, Harney County, Oreg.; and Eureka Mills, Cal., all
                    complaining that there is no one at these places to register them.</p>
                <closer><signed>
                        <list>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Li Yung Yew.</hi></item>
                            <item><hi rendition="#small-caps">Chang Ting Chip.</hi></item>
                        </list>
                    </signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
